  "live_records": {
    "q-341195": {
      "dom_id": "step-q341195",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-4{x}^{2}+1$ defined on $[-2,2).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension of $G(x)=-8x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-4{x}^{2}+1∼-\\frac{13}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{64(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-8x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-\\frac{13}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{64(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-2,2),$\n\n- continuous on $[-2,2),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-2)=F(2),$ if we extend the value at $x=2$ using the formula $F(x)=-4{x}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the $4$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4{x}^{2}+1) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{13}{3})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{64(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ -8x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{64(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ -8x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\ -8x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
